    "body": "Adds ADR-015, which decides how text wraps within a container and how runs of whitespace collapse to a single space.\n\n## The gaps this fixes\n\nToday, text does not wrap. A paragraph longer than its container extends past the right edge and past the viewport. Firefox renders the same paragraph in multiple lines within its box. A browser that clips long paragraphs is not a browser.\n\nSeparately, whitespace does not collapse. HTML's default `white-space: normal` collapses runs of spaces, tabs, and newlines into one space. HoneyBee renders them verbatim, so source-indented HTML produces irregular spacing.\n\n## The decision\n\nThree coordinated changes in the layout pipeline:\n\n1. **Whitespace collapsing** per CSS `white-space: normal`.\n2. **Line breaking** at word boundaries, greedy fill, using the existing `TextMeasurer` seam.\n3. **Direction-aware** line layout: first-strong heuristic detects RTL (Arabic, Hebrew, Syriac, Thaana, NKo, Adlam) vs LTR.\n\n## Why direction is in v1, not deferred\n\nNaive LTR-only wrapping would regress Arabic rendering. Consider a text node containing `Hello عربي world`:\n\n- **Correct bidi rendering** (what a browser should produce): the LTR words keep their positions, the RTL segment shapes correctly within its slot.\n- **Naive LTR wrapping**: splits at spaces and lays words left-to-right, so any RTL segment ends up in the wrong visual order relative to its context.\n\nFor a paragraph that is entirely Arabic, the problem is subtler but real: without direction detection the wrapper has no basis for right-aligning line fragments, so lines would be left-aligned with words in reading order -- which is correct for the words but wrong for the page's overall visual structure.\n\nGiven HoneyBee's mission is \"Fast websites for Egypt,\" shipping a wrapping regression on Arabic text is not acceptable. The first-strong heuristic is ~30 lines of codepoint range checking. It handles single-direction paragraphs correctly, which is the common case.\n\n## Data model\n\n`LayoutText` becomes multi-line:\n\n- Holds `std::vector<LineFragment> lines`\n- Each `LineFragment { x, y, width, height, text }`\n- `bool rtl` records the detected direction\n- Bounding box `width` = max line width; `height` = sum of line heights\n- Paint iterates fragments, drawing each on its own baseline\n\n## Deferred\n\n- `display: inline` (ADR-016+; still block-only)\n- Full UAX #9 bidi reordering (mixed LTR/RTL within one text node)\n- `white-space: pre` / `pre-wrap` / `pre-line`\n- `line-height` property\n- `text-align: center` / `right` / `justify`\n- Hyphenation, `word-break`, `overflow-wrap`, `hyphens`\n- Character-level breaking for CJK\n- `dir` attribute / `direction` CSS property\n- Orphan/widow control\n\n## Reusing the existing seam\n\nLayout already has a `TextMeasurer` callback (production: Pango; tests: fake). Line breaking needs to measure individual words. That is exactly what the seam does. No new dependencies, no new test bypass, and line-breaking unit tests remain headless-CI compatible.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `src/renderer/layout/layout_box.hpp` -- `LineFragment` and updated `LayoutText`\n- `src/renderer/layout/layout.cpp` -- whitespace collapsing, direction detection, line breaking\n- `src/renderer/paint/paint.cpp` -- iterate line fragments\n- `src/browser/pango_text.cpp` -- `LineFragment`-aware drawer\n- `tests/test_layout.cpp` -- new tests for line breaking (LTR + RTL), whitespace collapsing, direction detection\n- `tests/test_paint.cpp` -- one test for multi-line text draw positions\n\nNo changes to `renderer::css`, `renderer::dom`, `renderer::html`.\n\n## Next\n\nOnce ADR-015 merges, the implementation issue will be \"Implement text wrapping and whitespace collapsing per ADR-015\" -- split into three commits (whitespace collapsing + direction detection, then line breaking, then paint multi-line), same rhythm as #116 and #121.\n",
